WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:09.197
I set off on the four-mile walk to our forge, thinking about all I had seen

2
00:00:09.198 --> 00:00:13.380
and about the fact that I was a common laboring boy, whose boots were thick and hands

3
00:00:13.381 --> 00:00:17.793
were coarse. When I reached home, my sister and Mr. Pumblechook wanted to know all about

4
00:00:17.794 --> 00:00:23.102
my visit. But I thought if I described Miss Havisham and her house my sister would

5
00:00:23.103 --> 00:00:25.785
not understand. So I said as little as I could, but this was not enough for them,

6
00:00:25.786 --> 00:00:30.356
and they badgered me to tell them more. I finally began making up stories. "Now, boy,"

7
00:00:30.357 --> 00:00:32.944
said Mr. Pumblechook, "what was Miss Havisham like?" "Very tall and dark," I answered. "And what

8
00:00:32.945 --> 00:00:35.261
was she doing when you arrived?" "She was sitting in a black, velvet coach." My sister and

9
00:00:35.262 --> 00:00:37.856
Mr. Pumblechook stared at each other. "She was?" they said. "Yes, and Miss Estella—that's her niece,

10
00:00:37.857 --> 00:00:43.269
I think—handed her cake and wine on a gold plate," I said. "Was anyone else there?"

11
00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:49.579
asked Mr. Pumblechook. "Four huge dogs. And they fought for veal cutlets out of a silver basket."

12
00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:52.842
"Where was this coach?" asked my sister. "In the house. But there weren't any horses with

13
00:00:52.843 --> 00:00:57.325
it." They then discussed if this were possible, and Mr. Pumblechook concluded that I had mistaken

14
00:00:57.326 --> 00:01:00.420
a sedan chair for a coach. "Miss Havisham is odd, you know, odd enough to spend her

15
00:01:00.421 --> 00:01:03.817
days in a sedan chair." "And we played with flags," I went on. "Estella waved a

16
00:01:03.818 --> 00:01:09.913
blue flag, and I waved a red one, and Miss Havisham waved one sprinkled all over

17
00:01:09.914 --> 00:01:14.310
with little gold stars." At this point Joe came in from the forge for tea, and they

18
00:01:14.311 --> 00:01:24.055
told him what I had seen. He opened his blue eyes in amazement. I was overcome

19
00:01:24.056 --> 00:01:29.055
with remorse, but only toward Joe. After Mr. Pumblechook had left and my sister was washing

20
00:01:29.056 --> 00:01:34.380
up, I crept back to the forge to sit with Joe while he worked. "Joe, before the

21
00:01:34.381 --> 00:01:40.643
fire goes out, I'd like to tell you something. Remember everything I said about Miss Havisham's?"

22
00:01:40.644 --> 00:01:47.451
"Remember? It was wonderful." "It was all lies." "All of it? You mean there weren't even

23
00:01:47.452 --> 00:01:50.039
any veal cutlets or dogs?" "No dogs. It's terrible, Joe, isn't it?" "Terrible!" cried Joe. "What possessed

24
00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.777
you to tell such lies?" And then I told him that I felt miserable. That Mr.

25
00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:56.213
Pumblechook and Mrs. Joe had harassed me until I felt I had to say something. That

26
00:01:56.214 --> 00:01:59.436
there was a beautiful girl at Miss Havisham's who said I was common. And that I knew

27
00:01:59.437 --> 00:02:04.111
I was common and wished I wasn't, and somehow the lies were all connected to this,

28
00:02:04.112 --> 00:02:10.865
but I didn't really understand how. Joe thought for a minute. "There's one thing you can

29
00:02:10.866 --> 00:02:14.769
be sure of: lies are lies. Don't tell any more, Pip. That's not the way to get

30
00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:21.928
out of being common. As for her saying you're common, I don't understand that at all.

31
00:02:21.929 --> 00:02:31.682
She must not know that you are an uncommon scholar." "No. I am ignorant and backward,

32
00:02:31.683 --> 00:02:35.182
Joe." I shook my head. "You're not angry with me?" "No, old chap." I went up to

33
00:02:35.183 --> 00:02:38.333
bed that night thinking about how common Estella would consider Joe with his thick boots and

34
00:02:38.334 --> 00:02:44.571
rough hands. When I was older, I was to be apprenticed to Joe. In the meantime

35
00:02:44.572 --> 00:02:46.325
I did odd jobs around the forge, and I went to school in the village every evening.

36
00:02:46.326 --> 00:02:52.396
One of my fellow students was a girl named Biddy, an orphan like myself and the

37
00:02:52.397 --> 00:02:55.158
only one who ever learned anything. It soon occurred to me that the quickest way to

38
00:02:55.159 --> 00:03:00.650
make myself less common was to have her teach me everything she knew. A few nights later,

39
00:03:00.651 --> 00:03:06.817
I proposed this plan and she agreed. That night my sister had given me strict orders

40
00:03:06.818 --> 00:03:12.556
to retrieve Joe from the pub on my way home. I entered the pub and found

41
00:03:12.557 --> 00:03:15.968
Joe sitting at a table with Mr. Wopsle and a stranger. "Hello, Pip!" said Joe. The stranger

42
00:03:15.969 --> 00:03:20.888
turned to look at me and then motioned for me to sit in the empty spot

43
00:03:20.889 --> 00:03:25.293
beside him. "No, thank you," I said and found a seat next to Joe on the

44
00:03:25.294 --> 00:03:27.627
opposite side of the table. The stranger nodded to me and rubbed his leg in an odd

45
00:03:27.628 --> 00:03:32.381
way. He offered to buy Joe and Mr. Wopsle something to drink. All three men ordered

46
00:03:32.382 --> 00:03:37.452
rum and water, and then the stranger said, "I'm not acquainted with this area, but it

47
00:03:37.453 --> 00:03:41.357
seems like desolate country toward the river." "Most marshes are desolate," said Joe. "No doubt. Do you

48
00:03:41.358 --> 00:03:44.452
find gypsies or vagrants of any sort out there?" "No one but a runaway convict now

49
00:03:44.453 --> 00:03:52.765
and then, and we don't find them so easily. Eh, Mr. Wopsle?" The church clerk grunted

50
00:03:52.766 --> 00:03:59.670
in agreement. All this time the stranger kept looking at me. But he said nothing to me

51
00:03:59.671 --> 00:04:05.757
until the glasses of rum and water were brought. And then he conveyed his message in

52
00:04:05.758 --> 00:04:10.662
silence. He stirred his drink not with a spoon but with a file! He did this

53
00:04:10.663 --> 00:04:14.154
so that nobody but me saw him, and then he wiped the file and put it in

54
00:04:14.155 --> 00:04:20.408
his breast pocket. As soon as I saw the file, I knew it was Joe's. I

55
00:04:20.409 --> 00:04:26.583
also knew that the stranger knew my convict. I gazed at him spellbound, but he sat

56
00:04:26.584 --> 00:04:32.233
there taking little notice of me and talking of turnips. A half-hour passed and Joe got up

57
00:04:32.234 --> 00:04:37.884
to leave. "Wait a moment, Mr. Gargery," said the stranger. "I think I've got a bright

58
00:04:37.885 --> 00:04:40.567
new shilling somewhere in my pocket, and if I do, the boy shall have it." He

59
00:04:40.568 --> 00:04:48.051
took a coin from a handful of small change, wrapped it in some crumpled paper, and gave

60
00:04:48.052 --> 00:04:55.217
it to me. "Yours!" he said. "Thank you." I held tight to Joe's hand. When we

61
00:04:55.218 --> 00:04:58.122
arrived home, my sister was not in a very bad mood, so Joe decided to tell

62
00:04:58.123 --> 00:05:03.709
her about the shilling. "A counterfeit one, I bet," said Mrs. Joe triumphantly, "or he wouldn't have

63
00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:11.376
given it to the boy. Let's have a look." I took the coin out of the

64
00:05:11.377 --> 00:05:15.122
paper and it proved to be a genuine one. "But what's this?" said Mrs. Joe, throwing

65
00:05:15.123 --> 00:05:22.606
down the shilling and picking up the paper. "Two one-pound notes?" Joe quickly ran back to the

66
00:05:22.607 --> 00:05:26.281
pub to try to return them to their owner. While he was gone, I sat on

67
00:05:26.282 --> 00:05:31.360
my stool near the kitchen fire, feeling sure the man would not be there. Joe soon

68
00:05:31.361 --> 00:05:36.527
came back, saying that the stranger was gone, but he had left the man a message at

69
00:05:36.528 --> 00:05:39.685
the pub. My sister took the notes and put them in a teapot in the parlor.

70
00:05:39.686 --> 00:05:42.598
There they remained a nightmare to me for many days. I couldn't sleep that night, thinking

71
00:05:42.599 --> 00:05:45.423
of the stranger. I also thought what a coarse and common thing it was to be on

72
00:05:45.424 --> 00:05:54.246
secret terms with convicts—a part of my life that I had managed to forget until now.

